From: mscharged-port contributors
Subject: [PATCH] Draw a host overlay over the native VI desktop picture
Upstream-base: d664382f57002fbff143911c81b06b7e7a4b3ab4
Classification: host presentation (frame-rate counter). Apply after 0089.

The port's optional frame-rate counter (display.fps_overlay) has to be visible
in fullscreen, where the window title is not. The native VI output presents
the game's picture itself in one surface pass; Aurora's ImGui pass exists only
in its own end_frame, which the native path never uses.

aurora_imgui_begin_overlay/end_overlay record ImGui draws in surface pixels
and publish them. Scanout draws the latest overlay after the picture, in the
same surface pass, while the acquired surface has the size the overlay was laid
out for; after a resize it waits for the host's new layout. Screenshots
re-render the picture without it, and the game's EFB, XFB and frames never
contain it.

The overlay frame only draws: no ImGui platform backend frame (which would
show or change the OS cursor, capture the mouse while dragging and open
gamepads), no mouse or keyboard input and no imgui.ini. Font changes go
through aurora_imgui_update_overlay_fonts, which drains queued presentations
and rebuilds the renderer's font texture on the render worker.

diff --git a/include/aurora/imgui.h b/include/aurora/imgui.h
index 77eba9a..b5e877c 100644
--- a/include/aurora/imgui.h
+++ b/include/aurora/imgui.h
@@ -13,6 +13,21 @@ extern "C" {
 
 ImTextureID aurora_imgui_add_texture(uint32_t width, uint32_t height, const void* rgba8);
 
+// Host overlay over the native VI desktop picture (a frame-rate counter, for
+// example). Between begin and end the host records ImGui draws in surface
+// pixels; the frame reads no input, keeps no settings file and leaves the
+// cursor, mouse capture and gamepads alone. The recorded draws appear on every
+// following presented picture whose surface still has that size, until
+// replaced or cleared. Screenshots and the game's own frames never contain
+// them. Owner thread only.
+void aurora_imgui_begin_overlay(uint32_t surface_width, uint32_t surface_height);
+void aurora_imgui_end_overlay(void);
+void aurora_imgui_clear_overlay(void);
+// After the host built ImGui's font atlas (before the first overlay, and after
+// each change): clears the overlay, waits for queued presentations that may
+// still sample the previous font texture, then uploads the new one.
+void aurora_imgui_update_overlay_fonts(void);
+
 #ifdef __cplusplus
 }
 #endif
